<section>
    <h3>The handwritten mock</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php" data-trim data-line-numbers="1-14|4-9|8">
$client = new MockHttpClient([
    new MockResponse(
        json_encode([
            'id' => 'pay_123',
            'status' => 'succeeded',
            'amount' => 4200,
            'currency' => 'eur',
            // ... and the 40 other fields of the real response?
        ]),
        ['response_headers' => ['content-type' => 'application/json']],
    ),
]);

$service = new PaymentService($client);
    </code></pre>
    <p class="fragment subtitle">Tedious to write, and nothing guarantees it still looks like the API.</p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>Let's look at what a handwritten mock looks like, with what Symfony already gives us: <code>MockHttpClient</code> and <code>MockResponse</code>.</p>
        <p>[click] We build a JSON response by hand: an id, a status, an amount, a currency…</p>
        <p>[click] And then, the comment that hurts: "and the 40 other fields of the real response?" Because a real payment API response isn't four fields. It's forty, with nested objects, metadata, and dates in a very particular format. And we never copy all of it. We copy what we think we need today.</p>
        <p>[click] The result: it's tedious to write, and nothing guarantees it still looks like the API.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
